    "SU_0095": {
      "label": "Gantang",
      "type": "weight",
      "definition": "Measure of weight, with regional variations. On West and Mid Java it was regarded as 1/10 pikol (10 kati, around 12 pounds); on East Java only 5 kati. On Borneo gantangs were 4 1/2 pounds. In the regulations of Batavia it was stated as 13 1/3 pounds. In Mataram it was a weight of 8 pounds. On Ambon 17 1/2 pounds."
    },
    "SU_0098": {
      "label": "Ges",
      "type": "length",
      "definition": "(Dutch definition only in source)"
    },
    "SU_0106": {
      "label": "Kandijl",
      "type": "weight",
      "definition": "Load, as much as a team of oxen can carry; it was about 480 pond in Machilipatnam, in Golconda 520 pond. One kandijl = 4 picols = 1⁄5 last."
    },
    "SU_0116": {
      "label": "Last",
      "type": "weight",
      "definition": "(Dutch definition only in source)"
    },
    "SU_0124": {
      "label": "Man",
      "type": "weight",
      "definition": "In West Asia wellknown term for a unity of different weight. In Arabia one man was known as 2 5/8 Dutch pounds, but there were also man of 20, 24, 36, 53, 68 and 74 pounds. It differed not only by country, but also by type of goods. For example, the indigo was mostly sold in mans of 36, the sulphur in man of 68 pond. In the same country sometimes two different values for a man were used, so in Gujarat one big man of 36 1/2 pound and a small man of 34 1/2 pound. Regardless of the local value, one man was always divided into 40 ceer; 10 man was one frasel, 150 man was one bahar. The word was not from the Portuguese moa = hand, but has an old Mesopotamian origin and appears in classic greek and Latin as mina."
    },
