    "SU_0187": {
      "label": "Taël",
      "type": "weight",
      "definition": "A measure of weight as well as a measure of coinage in several Eastern countries, like China, Japan, Tonkin, Cambodja, Siam, Atjeh, Makassar, Bandjarmasin etc. As a weight measure the taël usually was 1/16 kati or about 37 1/2 grams. A weight like that formed the base for the coin system in Japan and was also called taël. This was divided in 10 maas = 100 condrin = 1000 rin (Japan had the decimal system). Until 1666 the Japanese taël was accounted for 57 stuiver, after that a decree by the Heren XVII instructed to fix the rate to 70 stuiver in order to bring some unification in the exchange of Indian coins. The Chinese taël was very similar to the Japanese, the value a little higher and set by the VOC to 80 stuiver. In Tonkin the taël was 1/16 kati as a weight measure; as a coin divided into 10 maas and 100 condrin, she was f 4,12 1/2 à f 4,25, because of higher silver level. In Siam the taël was as weight 1/20 kati or about 30 grams; as coin she was divided in 16 maas and 32 fuang. The value was over f 7. Also in Cambodja the taël was as weight 1/20 kati; as coin only f 3, because of less silver level. On Atjeh the golden taël was worth 4 rijksdaalder, divided into 16 golden maas. As silver coin there was also the Spanish reaal à 60 stuiver, divided into 8 silver maas. Also on Makassar there were golden taëls, divided into 16 golden maas that"
    },
